\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The Spin image is the spinor-norm kernel}
\lean/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.range_spinToSpecialOrthogonal_eq_ker_spinorNorm}

\p{Let #{K} be a field in which #{2} is invertible, #{V} a
finite-dimensional #{K}-space, and #{Q} a nondegenerate quadratic form.
The Clifford norm of a Lipschitz lift, taken modulo squares, descends to
#{\nu_Q^O:O(V,Q)\to K^{\times}/(K^\times)^2}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.orthogonalSpinorNorm_lipschitzToOrthogonal}.
Its restriction to #{SO(V,Q)} is the spinor norm
#{\nu_Q:SO(V,Q)\to K^\times / (K^\times)^2}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.spinorNorm}. Then

##{\operatorname{im}\bigl(\Spin(V,Q)\to SO(V,Q)\bigr)
=\ker\nu_Q.}

Thus a special orthogonal transformation has a Spin lift exactly
when its spinor-norm class is trivial.}

\p{\citet{II.3.5--6, pp. 52--53}{chevalley1954algebraic} defines the
reversal norm and describes the reduced orthogonal image by square
classes of products #{Q(u)Q(v)}. His ordinary Clifford action and
reversal norm compare with TauCeti's twisted action on even lifts: grading
fixes such lifts, so the two actions agree, and reverse and star norms
coincide \ref{fcap-0028}. For a single nonisotropic reflection, the descended
orthogonal spinor norm is

##{\nu_Q^O(r_v)=[Q(v)]}

\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.orthogonalSpinorNorm_reflectionOrthogonal}.
This agrees with Chevalley's reverse norm. TauCeti's star norm on the odd
vector lift has class #{[-Q(v)]}; it is a different norm. The extra minus
sign cancels on an even product, where the reverse and star norms agree.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{Every Spin element has reverse norm one, so its image has trivial
spinor norm
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.spinorNorm_spinToSpecialOrthogonal}.
Conversely, suppose first that #{V} has positive dimension. Take
#{g\in SO(V,Q)} with #{\nu_Q(g)=1}, and choose a Lipschitz lift #{x} of
#{g}. Since #{\det(g)=1}, determinant parity first shows that
#{x} is even
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.mem_even_of_det_lipschitzToOrthogonal_eq_one}
\ref{fcap-001A}. Trivial spinor class says that
#{N_{\mathrm{rev}}(x)} is a square. Scalar normalization therefore produces
an even lift with reverse norm one
\href{https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/blob/1a573226a3d9f2c460a6cea6ff3cf2dc82b6a2a3/TauCeti/LinearAlgebra/CliffordAlgebra/Lipschitz/ReverseNorm.lean#L205-L226}{source theorem};
by the even-element comparison in \ref{fcap-0028}, it lies in Spin and has
the same orthogonal action. In dimension zero #{SO(V,Q)} is trivial, so the
image and the spinor-norm kernel are both its identity subgroup.}
}
